      "Language": "ita",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The authors report the case of a 57 year-old man with severe calcific aortic stenosis and single vessel coronary artery disease, symptomatic of angina pectoris, who refused surgical therapy and was treated with combined percutaneous aortic valvuloplasty and coronary angioplasty as a single procedure. Valvuloplasty was performed initially, and the calculated aortic valve area increased from 0.75 to 1.27 cm2. Subsequently, a 75% proximal right coronary artery stenosis was dilated to 20%. Two months later, at follow-up the patient was asymptomatic and the Doppler-determined aortic valve area was 1.01 cm2."},
